---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import data from '../data/pipeline.json';

const { destinations: d } = data;
const n = (v: number, d2 = 0) =>
  v.toLocaleString('en-GB', { minimumFractionDigits: d2, maximumFractionDigits: d2 });

const total = d.fixesWithADestination;
const of = (shape: string) => d.destinations.filter((x) => x.shape === shape);
const fixesOf = (shape: string) => of(shape).reduce((t, x) => t + x.fixes, 0);
const pct = (v: number) => (100 * v) / total;

const shapes = [
  ['UnLocode', 'A UN/LOCODE', 'Five characters: a country and a place. The standard, and the majority.'],
  ['FreeText', 'A place, in words', 'Written the way a person would say it out loud.'],
  ['ForOrders', 'No destination yet', '"For orders" — a real commercial state, not an omission.'],
  ['Route', 'A whole route', 'Two or more places and an arrow, in a field sized for one place.'],
  ['Unreadable', 'Debris', 'Characters no keyboard puts there on purpose.'],
] as const;

const top = d.destinations.slice(0, 20);
const forOrders = of('ForOrders');
const routes = of('Route').slice(0, 8);
const junk = of('Unreadable');
---
<Base
  title="Where they said they were going — 443 ways to fill in one field"
  description="The AIS destination field is free text, and it shows. UN/LOCODEs, port names in four languages, nautical nicknames, whole routes with an arrow, and 37 different ways to write 'I don't know yet'."
>
  <h1>443 ways to fill in one field</h1>

  <p class="lede prose">
    Position and speed are measured. The destination is <em>typed</em> — by a person, into a free
    text box, on a bridge, at sea. {n(d.vesselsTyping)} vessels filled it in across this window,
    and between them they produced <strong>{n(d.distinctStrings)} distinct strings</strong>.
  </p>

  <ul class="stats">
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(d.distinctStrings)}</b><span>distinct strings typed</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(d.distinctIgnoringSpacingAndCase)}</b><span>still distinct ignoring spacing</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(d.vesselsTyping)}</b><span>vessels that typed one</span></li>
    <li class="stat"><b>{n(d.vesselsThatChangedIt)}</b><span>ever changed it</span></li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What shape was it?</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    Every judgement below is about the <em>shape</em> of a string and never about which place it
    means. Deciding that <code>DKSKA</code> is LOCODE-shaped needs only the string; deciding that
    <code>DKSKA</code>, <code>SKAGEN</code> and <code>SKAW</code> are one harbour needs a gazetteer
    and an opinion about spelling, so that claim is not made here.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>shape</th><th class="n">strings</th><th class="n">fixes</th><th class="n">share</th><th>what it is</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {shapes.map(([key, label, note]) => (
          <tr>
            <td><strong>{label}</strong></td>
            <td class="n">{n(of(key).length)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(fixesOf(key))}</td>
            <td class="n">
              <span class="bar" style={`width:${Math.max(2, Math.round(pct(fixesOf(key)) * 1.6))}px`}></span>
              {n(pct(fixesOf(key)), 1)}%
            </td>
            <td class="wrap">{note}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note prose">
    Just over half the feed reached for the standard. The rest is language.
  </p>

  <h2>Skagen, in several hands</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    The northern tip of Denmark is where ships wait. It is also the clearest illustration of what a
    free text field does to a place name — a code, the Danish name, and the old English sailors'
    name for the same headland, all in the same week of the same feed.
  </p>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>as typed</th><th class="n">vessels</th><th class="n">fixes</th><th>shape</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {d.destinations
          .filter((x) => /^(DK ?SKA|SKAGEN|SKAW|OFF SKAW|SKAW EAST)$/i.test(x.text))
          .map((x) => (
            <tr>
              <td><code>{x.text}</code></td>
              <td class="n">{n(x.vessels)}</td>
              <td class="n">{n(x.fixes)}</td>
              <td>{x.shape === 'UnLocode' ? 'a code' : 'a name'}</td>
            </tr>
          ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note prose">
    Selected by matching the strings themselves, not by looking up a port. A reader who thinks
    <code>SKAW</code> belongs somewhere else is disagreeing with the caption, not with the data.
  </p>

  <h2>{n(forOrders.length)} ways to say "I don't know yet"</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    <strong>For orders</strong> is not a blank. It is a vessel that has sailed without knowing
    where it will discharge, waiting on the charterer to tell it — one of the most ordinary states
    in tramp shipping, and one the AIS destination field has no code for. So crews write it out,{' '}
    {n(forOrders.length)} different ways, on {n(fixesOf('ForOrders'))} fixes.
  </p>

  <p class="wordlist">
    {forOrders.map((x) => <code title={`${n(x.fixes)} fixes`}>{x.text}</code>)}
  </p>

  <p class="note prose">
    One of those is a typo for "Baltic" and stayed that way for {
      n(forOrders.find((x) => x.text.includes('BCLTIC'))?.fixes ?? 0)
    } consecutive position reports. Nothing in AIS spell-checks a destination, because nothing in
    AIS knows what a destination is supposed to look like.
  </p>

  <h2>A route, in a field sized for one place</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    {n(of('Route').length)} strings carry an arrow. The field holds one destination; a voyage has
    at least two ends, so crews put both in and point between them — in whichever direction seems
    natural, which is why both <code>&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;</code> appear.
  </p>

  <p class="wordlist">
    {routes.map((x) => <code title={`${n(x.fixes)} fixes`}>{x.text}</code>)}
  </p>

  <h2>The most-typed strings in the feed</h2>

  <div class="scroll">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="n">#</th><th>as typed</th><th class="n">vessels</th><th class="n">fixes</th><th>shape</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        {top.map((x, i) => (
          <tr>
            <td class="n">{i + 1}</td>
            <td><code>{x.text}</code></td>
            <td class="n">{n(x.vessels)}</td>
            <td class="n">{n(x.fixes)}</td>
            <td>{x.shape}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>And two that nobody typed</h2>

  <p class="prose">
    {n(junk.length)} strings out of {n(d.distinctStrings)} contain characters no keyboard produces
    on purpose — {junk.map((x, i) => (<>{i > 0 ? ' and ' : ''}<code>{x.text}</code></>))}. They are
    kept, like everything else here. A row that disappears without a record is a defect, and that
    does not stop being true because the row is embarrassing.
  </p>

  <blockquote class="prose">
    <p>
      None of this is a complaint about crews. The field is free text with no vocabulary, no
      validation and no code for the commonest answer in tramp shipping. People filled it in
      anyway, sensibly, in the language they had — and the pipeline's job is to report what they
      wrote rather than to tidy it into something that was never broadcast.
    </p>
  </blockquote>
</Base>
